From 2783b3539c0218d84035ec18bf33454f5f061beb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ken <kvandine@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 16:13:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] HID: xbox: split Xbox Wireless Controller support out of
 hid-microsoft

hid-microsoft binds the Bluetooth HID interface of the Xbox One S
(1708), Xbox Series X|S (1914) and Xbox Elite Series 2 (1797)
controllers, and the 8BitDo SN30 Pro+ in Xbox mode, only to add rumble
on top of hid-generic behaviour. Everything else in the driver is for
keyboards, mice, presenters and the Surface Dial.

Upcoming controller work (descriptor fixups, Share button and Elite
paddle mapping, trigger rumble, battery reporting) would keep piling
controller-only state into struct ms_data behind MS_QUIRK_FF. Move the
controllers into their own hid-xbox driver instead, so that work does
not touch the legacy Microsoft peripherals.

This is a straight move with no change in behaviour:

 - the same device IDs, report parsing, connect mask and rumble output
   report as before, and the same View/Xbox handling on classic
   firmware, still keyed on the descriptor layout;
 - CONFIG_HID_XBOX defaults to the value of CONFIG_HID_MICROSOFT, so
   `make olddefconfig` on an existing config keeps the controllers
   supported, built the same way (y or m);
 - module autoloading keeps working because hid-xbox now carries the
   hid:b0005g*v0000045Ep0000xxxx aliases that hid-microsoft drops.

The bound driver name changes from "microsoft" to "xbox". Anything that
matches DRIVER=="microsoft" for a controller, or blacklists
hid-microsoft to fall back to hid-generic, has to be updated.

HID_MICROSOFT no longer needs INPUT_FF_MEMLESS; HID_XBOX selects it.

Point the Xbox Bluetooth selftests at the new module and enable
CONFIG_HID_XBOX in the selftest config; the tests themselves are
unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01N6AAHtchJS5gBMxR7F1sf8
